    472	* **Route A^{(9)} (orthogonal corner, `W = 18`). DONE S137.**
    473	  Closed `exists_invertible_submatrix` for `(W = 18, d = j + 1)` via
    474	  six new declarations: `chiP_twenty_nine_eq_one`,
    475	  `chiP_forty_three_eq_one`, `chiP_one_hundred_seventy_nine_eq_one`,
    476	  `chiP_two_hundred_eleven_eq_one`, `chiP_two_hundred_ninety_three_eq_one`,
    477	  `exists_invertible_submatrix_W_eq_18_d_eq_j_plus_1`, and
    478	  `mps_bond_dim_W_eq_18_d_eq_j_plus_1 : (unfolding 18 (j+1) j).rank = 7`
    479	  for every `j ≥ 1`. All sorry-free; `#print axioms` confirms only
    480	  `propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound`. The matrix is `18^j × 18`;
    481	  live columns are `{0, 4, 6, 10, 12, 16}` (residues `1, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17
    482	  (mod 18)`), giving `φ(18) = 6` live columns. We need `R = 7`, so we add
    483	  one dead column with `chiP` non-zero — two candidates exist (`k = 1`
    484	  with `chiP 2 = 1` and `k = 2` with `chiP 3 = 1`); we pick `k = 1`.
    485	
    486	  **Triangulation via mixed leading/non-leading rows (W=18).**
    487	  The leading rows `{0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}` of the 18×18 slab admit no
    488	  triangulation; rows `2` and `5` have identical support at the 7 chosen
    489	  columns. Picking rows `{0, 1, 2, 6, 9, 11, 16}` works — in particular
    490	  rows `9, 11, 16` contribute primes `179, 211, 293` at distinct columns
    491	  while the leading rows `0, 1, 2, 6` cover the other four diagonal
    492	  primes `{2, 29, 43, 109}`. Permutation
    493	  `ρ ↦ (0, 2, 9, 1, 11, 6, 16)` and `σ ↦ (1, 6, 16, 10, 12, 0, 4)`
